<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><channel><language>en</language><title>All the news about Africa - RFI</title><description>Follow any political information, cultural, sporting and live streaming on RFI. The latest information, news and events in France, Europe and around the world.</description><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/</link><lastBuildDate>Sat, 29 Aug 2026 07:26:19 GMT</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/rss" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><category>Africa</category><title>Attackers target Niger's Niamey airport and presidency perimeter</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260829-attackers-target-niger-s-niamey-airport-and-presidency-perimeter</link><description>
Sustained gunfire and explosions rang out across several areas of Niger's capital, Niamey, early on Saturday, according to two Reuters witnesses, with the cause not immediately clear.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/8708e288-6390-11f1-9bdc-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/A%C3%A9roport-International-Diori-Hamani-de-Niamey-NIM-03.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/8708e288-6390-11f1-9bdc-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/A%C3%A9roport-International-Diori-Hamani-de-Niamey-NIM-03.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">423c7ca6-a377-11f1-a82a-25d87b3a8499</guid><pubDate>Sat, 29 Aug 2026 07:05:02 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/8708e288-6390-11f1-9bdc-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/A%C3%A9roport-International-Diori-Hamani-de-Niamey-NIM-03.jpg">© Wikimedia Commons CC BY SA 4.0 Barke11</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Algeria mourns wildfire victims as firefighters battle deadly blazes</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260828-algeria-mourns-wildfire-victims-as-firefighters-battle-deadly-blazes</link><description>
Algeria entered a second day of national mourning on Friday as firefighters continued to battle wildfires in the north of the country that have killed at least 12 people and injured dozens.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/ec57c822-a263-11f1-a811-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26239683873317.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/ec57c822-a263-11f1-a811-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26239683873317.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">7683abda-a2ad-11f1-95b2-8b24c41db7b3</guid><pubDate>Fri, 28 Aug 2026 10:01:16 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/ec57c822-a263-11f1-a811-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26239683873317.jpg">© AP</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Nigerian armed groups net nearly $6m in ransoms: study</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260826-nigerian-armed-groups-net-nearly-6-mn-in-ransoms-study</link><description>
Criminal groups in Nigeria raised nearly $6 million in ransom payments in the last year, a report said Wednesday, with two mass kidnappings by Boko Haram responsible for nearly 90 percent of the total.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/55d8be7a-a195-11f1-b953-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/6961939120bd9f4ef8b93ca57cd743048790f5a8.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/55d8be7a-a195-11f1-b953-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/6961939120bd9f4ef8b93ca57cd743048790f5a8.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">56fe9964-a195-11f1-a31a-95a8899ba87e</guid><pubDate>Wed, 26 Aug 2026 21:30:19 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/55d8be7a-a195-11f1-b953-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/6961939120bd9f4ef8b93ca57cd743048790f5a8.jpg">© Ifeanyi Immanuel Bakwenye / AFP/File</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Nigerian president orders rescue operation after mass kidnapping in mosque</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260826-nigerian-president-tinubu-orders-rescue-operation-after-mass-kidnapping-in-mosque</link><description>
Nigerian President Bola Tinubu has ordered the military and other security agencies to launch an immediate rescue operation after the mass kidnapping of worshippers during an attack on a mosque in the north-central Niger State last week.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/fba12020-32fd-11f0-a37e-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2025-04-29T164615Z_786983510_RC2N5X9GBHUH_RTRMADP_3_NIGERIA-SECURITY-MILITARY.JPG"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/fba12020-32fd-11f0-a37e-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2025-04-29T164615Z_786983510_RC2N5X9GBHUH_RTRMADP_3_NIGERIA-SECURITY-MILITARY.JPG" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">fd076dda-a114-11f1-8d4f-4d9d0ee435e5</guid><pubDate>Wed, 26 Aug 2026 06:19:34 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/fba12020-32fd-11f0-a37e-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2025-04-29T164615Z_786983510_RC2N5X9GBHUH_RTRMADP_3_NIGERIA-SECURITY-MILITARY.JPG">© REUTERS - Christophe Van der Perre </source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Namibia to rebury remains of political prisoners returned by South Africa</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260826-namibia-to-rebury-remains-of-political-prisoners-returned-by-south-africa</link><description>
Two Namibian colonial-era political prisoners, whose remains were returned by South Africa last week, are to be reburied in Namibia as part of Wednesday’s Heroes Day ceremonies. They are among a group of Namibians who died in South Africa under apartheid or during the colonial period and whose remains have been repatriated.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/bd65cd3e-12a5-11ea-9736-005056a99247/w:1024/p:16x9/2018-08-29t085135z_888509700_rc1fa2e0d7c0_rtrmadp_3_germany-namibia_0.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/bd65cd3e-12a5-11ea-9736-005056a99247/w:1024/p:16x9/2018-08-29t085135z_888509700_rc1fa2e0d7c0_rtrmadp_3_germany-namibia_0.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">83ec8b5a-9c9c-11f1-8280-f5365118855f</guid><pubDate>Wed, 26 Aug 2026 04:55:11 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/bd65cd3e-12a5-11ea-9736-005056a99247/w:1024/p:16x9/2018-08-29t085135z_888509700_rc1fa2e0d7c0_rtrmadp_3_germany-namibia_0.jpg">© Christian Mang/Reuters</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Survivors of Guinea's deadly landfill collapse say authorities ignored warnings</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260825-survivors-of-guinea-s-deadly-landfill-collapse-say-authorities-ignored-warnings</link><description>
Two days after a deadly landfill collapse in Guinea, rescuers are using giant excavators to search for victims. Anger is rising among residents, who say they had warned authorities that the rubbish dump was precarious.  
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/7284eb0e-9f36-11f1-b23d-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/000-C6BN4GG.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/7284eb0e-9f36-11f1-b23d-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/000-C6BN4GG.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4c158146-a06b-11f1-a6a2-f1db79bccd1e</guid><pubDate>Tue, 25 Aug 2026 12:10:39 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/7284eb0e-9f36-11f1-b23d-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/000-C6BN4GG.jpg">AFP - -</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Malian PM's visit to Algeria underlines need for mutual security goals</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260824-malian-pm-s-visit-to-algeria-underlines-need-for-mutual-security-goals</link><description>
The acting head of the Malian government, General Abdoulaye Maiga has begun a two-day visit to Algeria. It comes amid a thaw after months of tension. RFI spoke to Bakary Sambe, the Director of the Timbuktu Institute about the security situation and what the countries hope to gain from the meeting.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/626a8dea-9fc3-11f1-aa7c-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/AP22312578792228-Abdoulaye-Ma%C3%AFga.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/626a8dea-9fc3-11f1-aa7c-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/AP22312578792228-Abdoulaye-Ma%C3%AFga.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">83603542-9fc1-11f1-8b4e-e347e9354ddc</guid><pubDate>Mon, 24 Aug 2026 15:25:14 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/626a8dea-9fc3-11f1-aa7c-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/AP22312578792228-Abdoulaye-Ma%C3%AFga.jpg">AP - Peter Dejong</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Six million Somalis face acute hunger as drought and conflict worsen</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260824-six-million-somalis-face-acute-hunger-as-drought-and-conflict-worsen</link><description>
Six million people in Somalia are facing acute hunger, with nearly two million children at emergency levels, as severe drought, conflict and falling humanitarian funding deepen what the UN describes as one of the world’s worst malnutrition crises. Medical charity MSF tells RFI it's sounding the alarm "before it's too late".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/089fa040-9f80-11f1-979d-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26232458355606-1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/089fa040-9f80-11f1-979d-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26232458355606-1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e9ca8582-9f97-11f1-9ee8-75486ab540dd</guid><pubDate>Mon, 24 Aug 2026 10:40:55 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/089fa040-9f80-11f1-979d-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26232458355606-1.jpg">©  Farah Abdi Warsameh / AP Photo</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Violence against healthcare workers in DRC hampers Ebola response</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260824-violence-against-healthcare-workers-in-drc-hampers-ebola-response</link><description>
Healthcare workers in the Democratic Republic of Congo have been coming under attack, the United Nations and Red Cross have warned, hampering efforts to stem the spread of the Ebola virus, which is spreading 'exponentially'.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/7a5c8358-9d67-11f1-b9dd-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-31T085519Z-246925543-RC23HLAKM9NK-RTRMADP-3-HEALTH-EBOLA.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/7a5c8358-9d67-11f1-b9dd-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-31T085519Z-246925543-RC23HLAKM9NK-RTRMADP-3-HEALTH-EBOLA.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">cd402608-9f9f-11f1-896c-8324a3ed262e</guid><pubDate>Mon, 24 Aug 2026 10:08:11 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/7a5c8358-9d67-11f1-b9dd-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-31T085519Z-246925543-RC23HLAKM9NK-RTRMADP-3-HEALTH-EBOLA.jpg">© Gradel Muyisa Mumbere/Reuters</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>DRC and M23 rebels agree on roadmap for peace in eastern Congo</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260823-drc-and-m23-rebels-agree-on-roadmap-for-peace-in-eastern-congo</link><description>
The Democratic Republic of Congo government and the M23 rebel group have agreed on a roadmap for peace talks aimed at resolving the protracted conflict in eastern DRC. It marks the latest step in an internationally brokered peace process that has seen the parties make cautious concessions, without ending the fighting.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/8dd2067c-9ef4-11f1-b3c6-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26177576707008.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/8dd2067c-9ef4-11f1-b3c6-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26177576707008.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">57463c2a-9ef6-11f1-9684-4b4489a4c262</guid><pubDate>Sun, 23 Aug 2026 15:00:52 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/8dd2067c-9ef4-11f1-b3c6-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26177576707008.jpg">© AP Photo/Moses Sawasawa</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Mystery elephant deaths in Kenya leave experts divided over cause</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260823-mystery-elephant-deaths-in-kenya-leave-experts-divided-over-cause</link><description>
Conservationists and Maasai communities are calling for urgent answers as paralysis-like symptoms spread among elephants in Kenya's Amboseli National Park, with competing theories emerging over what is causing their deaths.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/38c7617a-9bd1-11f1-889b-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Genghis-Khan-Elephant-Kenya.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/38c7617a-9bd1-11f1-889b-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Genghis-Khan-Elephant-Kenya.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">6595647c-9bcc-11f1-8fd9-dfc10b227463</guid><pubDate>Sun, 23 Aug 2026 13:13:57 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/38c7617a-9bd1-11f1-889b-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Genghis-Khan-Elephant-Kenya.jpg">© SIMON MAINA / AFP</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Togo says it has 'serious' evidence against detained French journalists</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260823-togo-says-it-has-serious-evidence-against-detained-french-journalists</link><description>
Togo's government says it has "serious and consistent evidence" against two French documentary filmmakers detained since July on charges of making false statements. Gaël Mocaër and Sebastian Perez Pezzani were arrested along with their Togolese fixer, Fred Vedomey, and have denied wrongdoing.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/5bf99606-5008-11eb-ae90-005056a964fe/w:1024/p:16x9/000_Par1348444.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/5bf99606-5008-11eb-ae90-005056a964fe/w:1024/p:16x9/000_Par1348444.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">fdddc9cc-9ec9-11f1-9875-e5f10da2b52b</guid><pubDate>Sun, 23 Aug 2026 08:56:54 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/5bf99606-5008-11eb-ae90-005056a964fe/w:1024/p:16x9/000_Par1348444.jpg">© AFP/EMILE KOUTON</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>'An invisible avalanche': what happened in Cameroon's 1986 Lake Nyos disaster?</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260821-an-invisible-avalanche-what-happened-in-cameroon-s-1986-lake-nyos-disaster</link><description>
On 21 August, 1986, 1,746 people died from carbon dioxide poisoning after gas escaped overnight from the bottom of Lake Nyos in Cameroon. RFI examines what happened in the world's deadliest "limnic eruption" and how, 40 years later, our understanding of the danger posed by volcanic lakes is still evolving. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/6970f48a-1541-11ea-b2e0-005056bf7c53/w:1024/p:16x9/lake_nyos_0.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/6970f48a-1541-11ea-b2e0-005056bf7c53/w:1024/p:16x9/lake_nyos_0.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">9f1af974-9d4c-11f1-8f6e-f35f06fd4b75</guid><pubDate>Fri, 21 Aug 2026 14:47:55 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/6970f48a-1541-11ea-b2e0-005056bf7c53/w:1024/p:16x9/lake_nyos_0.jpg">Wikipedia/Domaine public</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>DR Congo Ebola outbreak 'growing exponentially' with more than 2,500 dead</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260821-dr-congo-ebola-outbreak-growing-exponentially-with-more-than-2-500-dead</link><description>
The Ebola outbreak raging in the Democratic Republic of Congo is growing "exponentially", with more than 2,500 deaths recorded and half of those in the last 20 days, the senior UN coordinator on the disease said Friday.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/30aa3c84-927a-11f1-8c5b-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/4c0d8f36aaec52efd39cb5a048c64eb1b85d0d42.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/30aa3c84-927a-11f1-8c5b-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/4c0d8f36aaec52efd39cb5a048c64eb1b85d0d42.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">837f67ba-9d54-11f1-9288-2deea6adc246</guid><pubDate>Fri, 21 Aug 2026 11:36:12 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/30aa3c84-927a-11f1-8c5b-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/4c0d8f36aaec52efd39cb5a048c64eb1b85d0d42.jpg">© Jospin Mwisha / AFP</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Cameroon’s President Biya home after 73-day absence, amid health speculation</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260821-cameroon-president-biya-home-after-73-day-absence-amid-health-speculation</link><description>
Cameroon’s President Paul Biya has returned to the country after spending 73 days abroad, ending an unusually long absence that has intensified speculation around the 93-year-old’s health and raised questions over Cameroon's political future.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/30b5a8f0-8b43-11f1-b9d1-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP-Sunday-Alamba-1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/30b5a8f0-8b43-11f1-b9d1-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP-Sunday-Alamba-1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">ff0dfbf0-9d34-11f1-a29e-831a4ab27004</guid><pubDate>Fri, 21 Aug 2026 10:11:10 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/30b5a8f0-8b43-11f1-b9d1-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP-Sunday-Alamba-1.jpg">AP - Sunday Alamba</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Deadly border mine collapse exposes risks behind Central Africa's gold rush</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260820-deadly-border-mine-collapse-exposes-risks-behind-central-africa-s-gold-rush-zamboye</link><description>
A deadly landslide at a gold mine on the Cameroon-Central African Republic border has triggered calls for greater oversight of mining operations, after dozens of people were killed and many more were reported missing.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/aa4723ae-7a99-11ef-9f4c-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/Gaga-mines-or.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/aa4723ae-7a99-11ef-9f4c-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/Gaga-mines-or.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">49f67a2a-9c90-11f1-ab31-a5221940408f</guid><pubDate>Thu, 20 Aug 2026 19:47:33 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/aa4723ae-7a99-11ef-9f4c-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/Gaga-mines-or.jpg">© RFI/Rolf Steve Domia-Leu</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Five years on from first vaccine, fight against malaria sees progress in Africa</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260820-five-years-on-from-first-vaccine-fight-against-malaria-sees-progress-in-africa</link><description>
On World Mosquito Day, attention remains focused on the devastating impact of the disease in Africa. The continent accounts for 95 per cent of malaria-related deaths worldwide. Five years after the roll-out of the first malaria vaccine, it is beginning to deliver measurable results – although significant challenges remain.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/b09af880-9c6f-11f1-a353-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25230427734255.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/b09af880-9c6f-11f1-a353-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25230427734255.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">1e5f87ce-9c92-11f1-bc8c-81338d49049e</guid><pubDate>Thu, 20 Aug 2026 15:23:43 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/b09af880-9c6f-11f1-a353-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25230427734255.jpg">© AP - Patrick Onen</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Rights group calls for release of two French journalists held in Togo</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260820-rights-group-calls-for-release-of-two-french-journalists-held-in-togo-ga%C3%ABl-moca%C3%ABr-sebastian-perez-pezzani</link><description>
Two French journalists detained in Togo since late July have been placed in pre-trial detention and charged with "false statements", Reporters Without Borders (RSF) has said, calling for their immediate release. 
